     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Sunday, March 1, 2015 at 3:38:15 PM UTC-5, Nathan L wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I would like to construct a binomial regression model in Stan using the Jeffery&#39;s prior (see <a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2680313/\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2680313/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792465090883000&amp;usg=AOvVaw36cgian_LVejDHCswDMYqS\">http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2680313/</a>) as part of my research efforts. I am not quite sure how to complete coding this model. Here is what I have so far.</div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>For any manual adjustment to the value of the log-posterior distribution, you need to use the increment_log_prob() function, which is documented in chapter 16 of the user manual. In addition, I doubt you want to do all that calculation in the transformed parameters block because it will all get stored. You might declare log_lp at the top of the transformed parameters block and then do all that calculation inside a local block. That would store log_lp but not all the intermediate stuff. Or just do it all within the model block but that won&#39;t store the contribution of the Jeffrey&#39;s prior. Finally, you want to use increment_log_prob(0.5 * log_determinant(<code><span style=\"color:#000\">XTWX))</span></code> rather than increment_log_prob(0.5 * log(determinant(<code><span style=\"color:#000\">XTWX)))<br><br></span></code>Ben<br><br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
